10
00:00:54,310 --> 00:00:55,990
This is Sam Tagert for SE Radio. I'm here today with Wesley Barry.

11
00:00:55,990 --> 00:01:04,469
Wesley Barry is co-founder of Anker and has worked at a variety of tech companies including Salesforce, Heroku, and Engineard as an engineer and developer advocate.

12
00:01:04,469 --> 00:01:07,030
Today we're going to talk about API design.

13
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:11,830
We've talked about various aspects of APIs in previous episodes such as 612 and 542.

14
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:15,030
We'll include links to those episodes as well as a few others in the show notes.

15
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,991
Welcome Wesley. Thanks. Thanks for having me.

16
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,950
Let's get started by defining what is an API.

17
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:37,350
I mean I think technically application protocol interface if I remember right that sounds right I mean really it's the interface that we expose to outside users right so most of my experience in particular is with HTTP driven APIs

18
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:47,350
and so usually it's somebody's making a request over the web to me that they want some work done in some way shape or form and that's how they tell me what and how I should do it.

19
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:53,991
So I think APIs actually exist at various levels because I think of an API as I've got a software module and the API is the things that I call. Yeah.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,910
To make it do things and also there's command line interfaces are also a form of an API because you can script them and make them programmatic.

21
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:05,749
And as you mentioned a lot of people talk about web interfaces as APIs.

22
00:02:05,759 --> 00:02:12,630
Yep. I've heard you in a talk before mention your own acronym for APIs called assumptions probably incorrect.

23
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,229
I found that rather funny.

24
00:02:14,229 --> 00:02:16,869
Can you talk about how you came up with that and what it means?

25
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:19,750
Sure.

26
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:27,750
I mean, it kind of speaks to, I suppose, all those different levels of APIs that you were mentioning, whether it's in a library, on the CLI, on the web,

27
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:36,631
that at least in my own experience, when I try to make a new interface for something, it's the sort of no plan survives contact with the enemy kind of thing. like I'll have a plan.

28
00:02:36,631 --> 00:02:48,869
I'll put together this interface and then I'll try to use it and realize that I've forgotten six or eight things or something that there's some edge cases that weren't considered that basically I'll end up with something that the experience isn't nearly what I hoped it would be that it just doesn't pan out.

29
00:02:48,879 --> 00:02:56,710
So it's one of the reasons why more and more I push towards trying to do lighter weight things I guess trying to basically have prototypes or other things that I can work against.

30
00:02:56,710 --> 00:03:04,309
Don't spend all the time to implement the interface front to back with full tests or something only to find out that I've made a huge error rate.

31
00:03:04,309 --> 00:03:11,190
So doing what I can to get more hands-on experience sooner because for me at least I find it really hard to just think through all of that in the abstract.

32
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:13,990
So that's kind of what I'm talking about with assumptions probably incorrect.

33
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:18,070
If I try to just do it abstractly upfront, I tend to miss things.

34
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:29,670
So I feel like really getting into the nitty-gritty of it and trying it and using it in anger as people say like actually using it the way you think a user would use it quickly reveals the things that you didn't think about or overlooked.

35
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:34,229
So if I understand correctly, you're advocating for more of an iterative or agile style of development.

36
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:37,830
Yeah, I definitely think that that makes a lot of sense in this context.

37
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:42,630
You can get a foundation of something together pretty quickly,

38
00:03:42,630 --> 00:03:51,910
but yeah, the edges always end up for me at least being pretty rough until I've had some real experience with it and can get some real feedback on what is working and what isn't working. So more agile is definitely the way to go.

39
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:57,509
Do you have any specific examples that come to mind of times when your assumptions were not quite correct?

40
00:03:57,509 --> 00:04:04,949
I just made a mistake related to that this morning when I was working on some API design.

41
00:04:04,949 --> 00:04:15,750
So I guess that's fresh where I was going in and we were working on something related to specifying domain names and what something can and can't do basically by which domains is able to operate on.

42
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:26,390
And I sort of quickly looked in the existing API and checked to see what precedents we might have and there was an example of an array of domain strings and I was like great I'll just use that.

43
00:04:26,390 --> 00:04:34,950
But it turns out that I had had a conversation with my colleague that I'd totally forgotten about in which he had pointed out that that's just like one of the things that we want to check against.

44
00:04:34,950 --> 00:04:38,551
And so I needed actually to have an array where there was both a type and a value. Right?

45
00:04:38,551 --> 00:04:43,510
So one of the possibilities is a DNS type with a domain name which is very similar to what was there already.

46
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:47,749
But just putting an array of domain names would have been too limiting and it would have been very difficult to change later.

47
00:04:47,749 --> 00:04:54,950
So just making that small tweak to something where there was a type and a value will give us a lot more flexibility in the future where again like

48
00:04:54,950 --> 00:05:03,749
I was just sort of assuming oh well I'll just follow the existing example not thinking about the broader implications in these other use cases that I just again didn't happen to be in mind at the time.

49
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:12,550
It can be hard to get all of the context all into your brain all at once sometimes I think with interfaces so you end up peacemeal recalling parts that you had previously forgotten.

50
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:21,430
That leads into my next question which you kind of already answered but the question was I've heard you talk about one of the major problems with API designs that were too close to the problem.

51
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:31,110
I think that's a good example right you're looking at the narrow thing of I just want to filter some domain names but you take a step back and you're like well maybe people would also want to filter other things.

52
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:32,790
Yeah.

53
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:41,270
And in that case, like the concrete example is we aren't going to support it right away, but we expect in the future people might want to filter on IP addresses instead of domain names, right?

54
00:05:41,270 --> 00:05:43,590
Like that's a very concrete way that that might flip.

55
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:47,510
But yeah, in terms of being too close to the problem, I think that comes up all the time, right?

56
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:54,469
Like you know the domain that your interface is for probably inside and out and oftent times users don't.

57
00:05:54,469 --> 00:06:04,469
And so even if you expose it in a way that seems straightforward to you, that's based on the assumption that they have a bunch of knowledge that they might not have or that they have a bunch of experience that they might not have.

58
00:06:04,469 --> 00:06:15,670
So I think in a lot of cases, you have to think about not just what technically can do all of the things, but what is going to be easy to discover or easy to understand or easy to pick up if you've never done this before.

59
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:20,870
And sometimes maybe that means parts of the interface that are just for brand new users, right?

60
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:28,390
that maybe you don't expect experienced users will really use it that much at all, but it provides some extra support basically for people that don't have the experience yet to help get them to that point.

61
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:32,710
Maybe like a really quick upand getting started script.

62
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:34,309
What pops into my mind is wire guard.

63
00:06:34,309 --> 00:06:42,550
I don't know if you've used it, but there's like a wire guard quick up or something like that that you just run it and it just does the thing, but then more advanced users might want to specify more parameters.

64
00:06:42,550 --> 00:06:44,309
Does that kind of fall into your bucket?

65
00:06:44,309 --> 00:06:50,870
Yeah, I think that's a great example where there is an easy way, but it's not the way for everyone necessarily.

66
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:58,950
I think at least in the past, I've sometimes gotten caught up in this like try to make something that will be technically good enough for everyone,

67
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:03,990
but then because it's so flexible and perhaps so complicated, it ends up kind of being not that good for anyone.

68
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:05,589
It's like too confusing.

69
00:07:05,589 --> 00:07:12,550
Yeah, I know what I want to do, but I can't figure out how to do it with this thing because this thing is trying to let me do any possible thing, and I don't want to do any possible thing.

70
00:07:12,550 --> 00:07:14,469
I want to do this one narrow thing, right?

71
00:07:14,469 --> 00:07:21,189
So sometimes providing something that just does that narrow thing if there's enough users that need and want that can be a better experience than the something for everyone.

72
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:24,790
I find software juders never have a problem introducing more complexity.

73
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:29,510
That's for sure. Yeah.

74
00:07:29,510 --> 00:07:31,510
We talked about agile/iterative development.

75
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:34,230
What are the best ways you found to get feedback from users?

76
00:07:34,230 --> 00:07:41,749
I think it's a bit of a tangent, I guess, but for a little while, I hope to maybe come back to this at some point, but for a little while, I was working on a board game.

77
00:07:41,749 --> 00:07:49,990
And there's something that I learned from that context, from the advice that I heard from other board game designers, which is one of the best things you can do is what they call a blind play test.

78
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:57,350
The idea is basically you sit somebody down with this board game that you're working on and give them the rule book and say, "Please try to figure it out.

79
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:04,150
I'm just going to watch what you're doing and I'm not going to answer your questions because if I do, it will sort of invalidate the test.

80
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:05,909
I want to just know if you're able to figure this out.

81
00:08:05,909 --> 00:08:09,350
I think in a lot of cases with APIs like the same kind of thing if you can manage it.

82
00:08:09,350 --> 00:08:13,189
This is similar to other UX testing things that people sometimes do.

83
00:08:13,189 --> 00:08:15,189
Here is the documentation for this interface.

84
00:08:15,189 --> 00:08:18,309
Here's the interface.

85
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:20,550
Maybe here is the task that I want you to try to accomplish.

86
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:26,550
Just speak as though you're thinking aloud and try to accomplish that. I just want to see what happens, right?

87
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:29,029
And then you can see, oh, that's interesting.

88
00:08:29,029 --> 00:08:31,749
I did this with five different people and all of them got stuck on this one thing.

89
00:08:31,749 --> 00:08:35,190
probably means that there's something that isn't quite right there, right?

90
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:40,389
Or when they were talking about how they were thinking about it, they all use different terminology than I would use. Like that's interesting.

91
00:08:40,389 --> 00:08:47,910
Maybe that means that the way that the documentation is written isn't quite right because my user base and me don't have quite the same terminology for this thing. Or maybe I should mention both terms.

92
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:58,470
Things like that. Like there's a lot of things that become revealed that other things don't seem to work as well for because whether you want to or not, if you're answering questions and really interacting with someone, you're kind of like leading the witness, right?

93
00:08:58,470 --> 00:09:08,070
So, like if you notice that it seems like they're struggling a little bit, you'll be likely to offer them help and then that's great for in the moment, but it turns out that if everybody else is going to struggle at that point,

94
00:09:08,070 --> 00:09:11,990
you're usually not going to be in the room to notice that they're having a hard time and help them through it, right?

95
00:09:11,990 --> 00:09:18,150
So, yeah, I think it can be challenging to get in that position basically and say, "I'm sorry.

96
00:09:18,150 --> 00:09:21,269
This is maybe not going to be very fun for you, but it's going to be very valuable.

97
00:09:21,269 --> 00:09:27,990
Like, can you please just try to struggle through it a little But, you know, I'm not going to leave you struggling forever, but I do want you to try to push through at least a little.

98
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:34,230
I wonder if it might be worth recording those so that there's nobody else in the room, so you're not tempted to influence the witness.

99
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:37,990
I definitely think so. If you have the setup for it, that's a great approach.

100
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:45,350
Sometimes APIs end up just being a simple CRUD type access for database type stuff.

101
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:46,949
What are the potential issues with that?

102
00:09:46,949 --> 00:09:48,470
And do you have any examples?

103
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,711
Sure. There's a couple things.

104
00:09:50,711 --> 00:09:54,550
one relates to some of what we were already talking about.

105
00:09:54,550 --> 00:10:07,590
So I think one of the dangers I guess with CRUD is that you get into that trying to have something that tries to cover every single case and so then some of the individual things can end up becoming incredibly complicated.

106
00:10:07,590 --> 00:10:17,990
As an example, I've seen and worked on APIs where update gets pretty overloaded where what update is actually doing depends a lot on which particular parameters you pass in, right?

107
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:28,069
And it's not necessarily just that behind the scenes a database record gets changed, but it might actually kick off other follow on operations and things like that depending on what the particular values are.

108
00:10:28,069 --> 00:10:39,030
That again can get really complicated and make it very difficult for both the implementers and operators as well as the people that are writing stuff to use it to just reason about what in the world actually happens when I call update.

109
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:41,110
That's like the dreaded it depends.

110
00:10:41,110 --> 00:10:46,711
I feel like the longer you've been in software, the more you've probably heard somebody say that, but it's sort of what happens when you call update. Well, it depends, right?

111
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:53,350
That's the buzzword of complicated software, I guess, in a lot of cases, complexity, you know?

112
00:10:53,350 --> 00:11:02,790
So, what I have liked in a lot of cases actually is to provide CRUD because you're going to need it, but then think about actually offering other actions or other capabilities for the resources.

113
00:11:02,790 --> 00:11:08,069
The other example is kind of like what you're talking about with WireGuard, right?

114
00:11:08,069 --> 00:11:13,590
Where you want to set up and tear down and see which WireGuard setups you have, but sometimes you want to just very simply turn it on, right?

115
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:17,829
and not necessarily worry about all the nitty-gritty of having created one and doing whatever was necessary, right?

116
00:11:17,839 --> 00:11:20,389
There are some other things like that too.

117
00:11:20,389 --> 00:11:22,230
Or another example is like with servers, right?

118
00:11:22,230 --> 00:11:26,310
If you want to reset a server, how are you supposed to do that if it's just CRUD?

119
00:11:26,310 --> 00:11:33,670
Do you call update and change this state to restarting or something like that feels a little bit awkward versus an action that just is please restart the server?

120
00:11:33,670 --> 00:11:47,590
So yeah, I think those are some of the edges definitely of like things that are not really just updating a record but actually cause something to occur and also potentially things that are more like workflow based or provide a particular use case some support.

121
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:54,710
Can you expand a little bit on separating actions from state and reading state versus doing things?

122
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:57,030
Sure.

123
00:11:57,030 --> 00:12:03,990
I mean, the way that I've tended to do it, which I feel like I've gotten in arguments with people about a lot, so I wouldn't say it's that popular, is in a standard sort of like REST interface,

124
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:13,910
a lot of times you'll see slash resources slash an identifier and then slash maybe restart or something for the server example, right?

125
00:12:13,910 --> 00:12:16,790
And what I like to do actually is to segregate those.

126
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:28,310
Like the kind of way that I like to think about it in a lot of ways is it's okay to do different things, but ideally each of the things that you're doing you do consistently and you make it clear where the separation is.

127
00:12:28,310 --> 00:12:34,710
And so what I like to do for something like that would be like slashservers/ ID slactions/reart.

128
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:42,470
And so then there's a clear sort of name space basically underneath a particular server where all of the actions live together.

129
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:47,591
And it makes it easier to understand and identify at a glance then that this is something that follows the action pattern. Right?

130
00:12:47,591 --> 00:12:51,110
So when you're documenting and stuff, you can basically say crowd operations work like this.

131
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:55,910
When you call update on a server, you should expect to get back a serialization of that server.

132
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:59,430
If you call read on a server, it should also be a serialization of a server.

133
00:12:59,430 --> 00:13:02,949
But actions on a server don't necessarily follow those rules, right?

134
00:13:02,949 --> 00:13:10,150
It might be you just get back a reference to a job that you can check on later or something else, not necessarily a server serialization, right?

135
00:13:10,150 --> 00:13:12,870
So again, like having that clear delineation that you know that it's separate.

136
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:23,670
The other thing that's nice is like if you have a nested resources where it might be like server/ something/v volume/ something by putting all of the actions together into one namespace,

137
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:27,110
it also helps to prevent polluting that subnamespace if that makes sense.

138
00:13:27,110 --> 00:13:33,671
So you don't have to worry about accidentally creating an action that has a name that conflicts with some other thing that you want to now nest there. Right?

139
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:41,110
all of the actions are together nicely of like here are all the methods you can call on this thing versus here are the sub resources or whatever if that makes sense.

140
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,030
That makes a lot of sense.

141
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:45,670
Another question just popped into my mind.

142
00:13:45,670 --> 00:13:47,350
You were talking about URLs.

143
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:49,190
How do you handle versioning?

144
00:13:49,190 --> 00:13:52,150
I know a lot of URLs tend to have like SLV1 or SLV2.

145
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:53,990
Is that a practice you recommend?

146
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:56,150
Is that something you caution against?

147
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:58,150
Boy, how long have you got?

148
00:13:58,150 --> 00:14:05,750
You know, it's one of those kinds of problems where I think it depends a lot on what you need or want.

149
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:11,990
So, first off, what I have done at least sometimes in the past is push for that to be more in the headers.

150
00:14:11,990 --> 00:14:19,590
It feels like from a semantics of HTTP perspective, that feels like a header kind of information, like it's a meta information about the request you're making.

151
00:14:19,590 --> 00:14:21,670
But almost everyone I've talked to does not like this idea.

152
00:14:21,670 --> 00:14:32,550
among other things like there's a lot of clients and stuff where headers are just a bit of a pain to use, especially if you're needing to use them on basically every request and putting in the path just is better for usability.

153
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:36,550
So more often than not, I have done it more in the path.

154
00:14:36,550 --> 00:14:47,189
I think the thing that gets really tricky is like if I had my way, I would prefer probably to version even different endpoints with different numbers instead of having to version the entire API, right?

155
00:14:47,189 --> 00:14:50,710
But that again becomes a usability nightmare basically instantly.

156
00:14:50,710 --> 00:14:56,470
But it's really hard because otherwise it's the sort of thing of like I made a change in this one endpoint that is a breaking change.

157
00:14:56,470 --> 00:15:00,470
I would like to roll this out but it's not backwards compatible.

158
00:15:00,470 --> 00:15:02,470
Do I have to roll a whole major version of this API?

159
00:15:02,470 --> 00:15:08,069
And if I do roll a whole major version of this API, do I need to also continue to operate the old version of the API?

160
00:15:08,069 --> 00:15:10,629
If so, for how long?

161
00:15:10,629 --> 00:15:15,590
Like an example of that is that I've always found interesting, but I don't know that I would want to do it is Stripe.

162
00:15:15,590 --> 00:15:19,509
At least historically, I believe their version numbers were all based on basically dates.

163
00:15:19,509 --> 00:15:29,750
And so when you first set up your Stripe integration, it would lock so that any API requests you make, unless you change this value, get the API as it was on that day basically.

164
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:36,150
And so they're not rolling out a new version every day or whatever, but it'll be whatever the most recent version was when you start using the API is the API you lock to.

165
00:15:36,150 --> 00:15:41,030
And then they try to basically maintain that API indefinitely.

166
00:15:41,030 --> 00:15:45,670
So you can at some point move up to a newer version or whatever, but the old version continues to be maintained.

167
00:15:45,670 --> 00:15:55,990
I mean that's where it gets really hairy really is once an API is out in the world that people are using making changes to it suddenly is much much more painful right like you can't go

168
00:15:55,990 --> 00:16:03,350
and change their code for them and so you don't want to cause them pain so yeah like versioning is in a lot of ways like to be avoided basically I think like as much as possible but obviously

169
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:13,430
you can't do that entirely what I've seen in a lot of cases I think that works okay is mostly focusing on major versions trying to only do non-breaking changes

170
00:16:13,430 --> 00:16:22,470
and then having a very long deprecation period if you do move to a new version and then doing probably like brown outs basically where you temporarily turn the API off so anybody that's still using it

171
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:31,350
that didn't see the announcements will get errors and have a chance to recover and respond to it before the API is totally gone but again it's pretty ownorous

172
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:38,069
so mostly I try to avoid it if I can which you can't really but as much as you can you try to avoid it it occurs

173
00:16:38,069 --> 00:16:46,550
to me that the biggest pain point might be changes on the back end that require you like you change the database so these two things are no longer associated how old API endpoints are trying to make that association.

174
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:48,471
It's no longer there. Is that something?

175
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,230
That kind of stuff can be a huge pain.

176
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:56,390
Like if you are in a situation where the back end has changed drastically and the API just doesn't quite match. Yeah.

177
00:16:56,390 --> 00:17:02,150
You end up basically having to maintain all kinds of things that have just logic to convert from one version to another.

178
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:12,549
Right. So I think in some cases I've kind of seen it's like here is the function that takes an API call from version one and translates it to version two and here's the call that translates it from version two to version three.

179
00:17:12,559 --> 00:17:21,510
and you kind of end up in this thing where it just sort of takes the request and chains it up through as many layers as it needs to to try to translate it into something that would be understood in the modern context.

180
00:17:21,510 --> 00:17:25,590
But of course maintaining the testing and infrastructure to actually operate all of those things is non-trivial.

181
00:17:25,590 --> 00:17:27,270
It's a real headache.

182
00:17:27,270 --> 00:17:32,950
I mean it's you could think about it, you know, with other software that you work on or whatever, right?

183
00:17:32,950 --> 00:17:40,549
It's like what if you had to maintain not only whatever the latest version was, but like every version of that you've ever written, right? It's a real headache. So you have to like make some trade-offs somewhere.

184
00:17:40,549 --> 00:17:44,470
like you can't really actually maintain every version.

185
00:17:44,470 --> 00:17:46,789
So, which ones do you choose to maintain or not?

186
00:17:46,789 --> 00:17:53,830
And how long do you continue to maintain the old ones before you just pull the plug and say we did what we could do, but if you are still aren't up to date, sorry. Like, it's just time to move on.

187
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:59,590
Another question that popped into my mind as we're talking about upgrading APIs and changing them.

188
00:17:59,590 --> 00:18:01,430
Have you used the strangler fig pattern?

189
00:18:01,430 --> 00:18:03,270
Does that sound familiar to you?

190
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:06,150
I have heard of it.

191
00:18:06,150 --> 00:18:09,110
I don't know if I've used it. I didn't call it that at the time, I don't think.

192
00:18:09,110 --> 00:18:14,549
I mean, it's kind of the idea that you remove more and more of the thing until there's almost none left, right?

193
00:18:14,549 --> 00:18:17,510
You kind of replace it with something else. Is that accurate or how would you describe it?

194
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:30,230
So, I would describe it as you've got your old back end and you've got a new back end and you kind of run them in parallel and compare the results and then ship the new one and then eventually after they're the same for a significant amount of time, then you remove the old one kind of thing.

195
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:31,910
Ah, yeah. Yeah, that makes total sense.

196
00:18:31,919 --> 00:18:41,990
I don't have that much experience doing that in particular, but I've seen that and I would definitely be interested in trying that approach if I was working on a problem like that.

197
00:18:41,990 --> 00:18:44,950
I just don't happen to have a problem like that that I've worked on recently at least.

198
00:18:44,950 --> 00:18:50,071
So, I've heard you use the term API connoisseur. What does that mean to you?

199
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:52,230
Sure.

200
00:18:52,230 --> 00:19:00,390
I mean, I think it's kind of like how sometimes I just drink a cup of coffee to drink a cup of coffee, but I'd say in general that's an area where I feel like I'm a bit of a connoisseur at least.

201
00:19:00,390 --> 00:19:09,510
Like I know some things about different regions that coffees are roasted in and some things about different ways that they might be fermented or roasted and I have some opinions about that.

202
00:19:09,510 --> 00:19:14,070
So when I have my cup of coffee or whatever, I'm thinking about like what is it that I like or dislike about this particular brew?

203
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:16,710
Would I want to do this again? Would I want to change something?

204
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:30,549
And so in the same way with APIs, I think I especially early on when I was working on them often would just be like, well, I have been assigned the task at my work of integrating with this thing, so I'm just going to do it, right? I'm just going to figure out what needs to happen.

205
00:19:30,549 --> 00:19:34,150
I'm going to do it and I'm going to move on, right?

206
00:19:34,150 --> 00:19:40,230
And later I eventually because I found it interesting got to more of a point of okay, like yes, I'm going to do that.

207
00:19:40,230 --> 00:19:42,549
But at the same time, I can say what of this API did I really like?

208
00:19:42,549 --> 00:19:44,870
What was interesting?

209
00:19:44,870 --> 00:19:54,230
what was a pain in the butt in the end when I started doing it like what are the parts that I like and dislike about this why do I like and dislike those and I think it's very helpful

210
00:19:54,230 --> 00:20:00,789
then to start to develop a sort of taste for it right so it's not just oh right well it's an API it is what it is I'm going to work with it and I'm going

211
00:20:00,789 --> 00:20:13,990
to move on right I think especially there's this challenge in that if you're designing APIs you don't get that many opportunities to actually do it basically like especially with web APIs I mean with other APIs you get a few more bites at the apple so to speak.

212
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:22,310
But like I think most people if you're designing web APIs, how many web APIs are you going to make in your whole career? Maybe six or eight or something at most.

213
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:24,310
Probably for a lot of people it's more like two or three.

214
00:20:24,310 --> 00:20:30,549
And so you're not going to have as many chances to make the mistakes and realize that you could or should have done it differently and try again.

215
00:20:30,549 --> 00:20:43,191
And so rather than doing that in the really costly way of just the APIs you produce, I think there's a lot of value in trying to do what you can to be critical and like learn from the APIs that are already out there to say what's an API you really like? Okay.

216
00:20:43,191 --> 00:20:48,390
why what are the things that you really like about it and help to get that to inform what you do in your own designs.

217
00:20:48,400 --> 00:20:53,270
So, as a connoisseur, do you have an example of a well done API that you really like?

218
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:56,549
Yeah.

219
00:20:56,549 --> 00:21:00,630
I mean, so in terms of my more recent work, a lot of what I've been working on has been CLI stuff.

220
00:21:00,630 --> 00:21:10,070
And so, an example of a CLI that I often will look to just to see how they did it and to get inspiration is the GH CLI, which is GitHub's CLI.

221
00:21:10,070 --> 00:21:19,830
And I mean, it's like anything when you're being critical of whatever, it's like I wouldn't do everything exactly the way that they did, but I mostly quite like what they did.

222
00:21:19,830 --> 00:21:30,711
And so, again, if I'm like, I think I want to maybe take from that example and do something similar to what they did, I'll run through that command six or eight times and with different parameters and try to really think about what is it that I actually like about this? What is the thing that I like?

223
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:33,750
Because I'm not implementing the exact same operation on my side, right?

224
00:21:33,750 --> 00:21:36,789
I'm just doing something that is maybe inspired by that, right?

225
00:21:36,789 --> 00:21:45,190
So getting a feel for it and then trying to bring that back and have something that also has some of the same I guess feel and same taste so to speak has been really productive.

226
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:48,390
Along those lines, is there a particular API you dread using?

227
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:54,710
I mean the most obvious example probably also from my recent work would be the OpenSSL CLI.

228
00:21:54,710 --> 00:21:56,311
Like what are the arguments? I don't even know.

229
00:21:56,311 --> 00:22:01,909
I have to look them up every time and then still have not the highest confidence that I have done the right thing.

230
00:22:01,909 --> 00:22:03,990
They're just very confusing, very cryptic.

231
00:22:03,990 --> 00:22:07,830
I guess you might say the one I find most frustrating I think is Git.

232
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:10,789
Git is pretty rough. I'm not going to lie.

233
00:22:10,789 --> 00:22:15,110
I mean that's come up before when I've talked to people of like I like what Git does.

234
00:22:15,110 --> 00:22:19,909
I don't necessarily like the interface that it provides to me to actually do it. For the core stuff it's fine.

235
00:22:19,909 --> 00:22:21,430
I've done it so many times that it's muscle memory almost.

236
00:22:21,430 --> 00:22:26,070
But for anything beyond that again it's like back to documentation and then I'm not sure that I did it correctly.

237
00:22:26,070 --> 00:22:37,830
I don't know how many rebases I've ended up messing up because yours and theirs don't mean what I think that they do and the order of things really matters and it's not super userfriendly despite being very powerful.

238
00:22:37,830 --> 00:22:46,789
So, the two things I dislike about it in particular, and you mentioned one of them, is the naming, the ours and theirs, but also reset,

239
00:22:46,789 --> 00:22:57,029
revert, and restore all kind of like set things back to a previous state, but in different ways, and it's very, it's nice that they're all the same cuz you remember they all set something back, but trying to remember which one sets what back is really hard.

240
00:22:57,029 --> 00:23:04,950
And the inconsistency as well, cuz I keep remembering that for branches and tags, you delete them, but for sure, remotes, you remove them.

241
00:23:04,950 --> 00:23:09,669
and like that I'm always like wait which one am I doing here? So those little polish things.

242
00:23:09,679 --> 00:23:22,470
Yeah. And it's something that I think about a lot when I'm working on interfaces of just like for me at least when I see something like that where it's in this one case it says remove and this other case it says destroy then I'm left thinking is there meaningful difference between these two?

243
00:23:22,470 --> 00:23:28,630
Is it just coincidence that they happen to have a different name or does remove really indicate something different than delete?

244
00:23:28,630 --> 00:23:30,710
You know maybe remove means that it's not sort of connected anymore.

245
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:34,310
There's not an association anymore but the object still exists.

246
00:23:34,310 --> 00:23:38,070
Or maybe it actually just deletes the object and they called it two different things for no apparent reason.

247
00:23:38,070 --> 00:23:42,630
It's like it makes me question how much I actually understand the interface in the first place, right?

248
00:23:42,630 --> 00:23:45,430
Which is not what you want your users to be doing, right? Do I even know what I'm doing here?

249
00:23:45,430 --> 00:23:49,029
Like I thought I did, but now you're making me question everything. That's the worst case really.

250
00:23:49,039 --> 00:23:54,310
I always go back to everything's done for a reason. It doesn't always necessarily make sense. It's not always a great reason.

251
00:23:54,310 --> 00:23:57,430
But when you don't know the reason, you're like left questioning, well, what was the reason?

252
00:23:57,440 --> 00:23:59,831
Yeah. Yeah.

253
00:23:59,831 --> 00:24:03,029
And can I ever understand what that reason was? Do I need to understand what that reason was?

254
00:24:03,029 --> 00:24:04,711
You know? Yeah.

255
00:24:04,711 --> 00:24:06,630
Is it important enough to waste time trying to figure it out?

256
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:11,510
Exactly. Speaking of surprises, what is the principle of least surprise?

257
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:16,470
I think it comes from Matts, the creator of Ruby.

258
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:21,750
So, a lot of my background is in Ruby programming and it's something that I've seen talked about in the context of Matt.

259
00:24:21,750 --> 00:24:31,830
So, basically like ideally your interface is set up in such a way that if you basically learn one part of it and you move to another part, you should be able to guess basically how things work.

260
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:37,590
So talking about our git example, you know, like delete versus remove, that violates, I guess, the principle of least surprise.

261
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:47,750
Ideally, like if you learn that getting rid of an object is done by calling delete and you move to a new object, the principle of least surprised should say basically you should be able to call delete and it should work, right?

262
00:24:47,750 --> 00:24:51,830
Or perhaps give you a meaningful message or instruct you as to how you should do it otherwise, right?

263
00:24:51,830 --> 00:24:55,669
Like you shouldn't be surprised of like wait a second like why is this called remove, right?

264
00:24:55,669 --> 00:24:59,029
Like that's just again not a good experience for the user.

265
00:24:59,029 --> 00:25:03,669
So trying to just minimize that as much as possible, make as much transfer between things as you can.

266
00:25:03,679 --> 00:25:07,430
To your comment about giving hints, I will give get credit.

267
00:25:07,430 --> 00:25:09,269
It's gotten better over the years at giving hints.

268
00:25:09,269 --> 00:25:13,830
Like when you're in the middle of a rebase or something, you can do the get status and it gives you little hints on what to do.

269
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:16,310
They're still kind of cryptic, but at least it's something.

270
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:22,550
I mean, that's been some of my worst experiences with various tools, I think, is, you know, the sort of you've done something wrong. Please try harder.

271
00:25:22,559 --> 00:25:25,350
Please try again. Right? It's like, well, what what do you mean?

272
00:25:25,350 --> 00:25:28,710
I I don't understand what I did wrong in the first place. What am I how am I supposed to deal with this?

273
00:25:28,710 --> 00:25:41,590
You know, my favorite programming language, Lab View, has a you get file not found error, but it never tells you what file it can't find, which is always interesting because you're like, uh, you know, you're looking for it, you can't find it. Why can't you tell me so I can go find it?

274
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,831
Yep. The dreaded something went wrong.

275
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:47,350
You're like, great, thanks for letting me know, I guess.

276
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:57,029
So, talking about principal lease surprise, there's this idea of interrogation versus implicit discoverability that seems kind of related. Can you talk about that a little?

277
00:25:57,039 --> 00:25:59,669
Sure.

278
00:25:59,669 --> 00:26:02,950
I mean, the idea with discoverability, I think, is more to that sort of like I guessed, right?

279
00:26:02,950 --> 00:26:13,750
I wasn't sure what this was going to be, so I tried a thing and it worked out versus more like I called a method and it listed to me all of the options and then I chose one of those options, right?

280
00:26:13,750 --> 00:26:15,270
So, it reveals to you the possibility. I don't know.

281
00:26:15,270 --> 00:26:21,750
I mean, this is one of the areas where I guess in some ways like I would rather not choose probably.

282
00:26:21,750 --> 00:26:23,669
It seems like a kind of improv theater both and situation, right?

283
00:26:23,669 --> 00:26:34,149
Like I think there are times where it's very helpful to be able to say, "Can you just tell me which operations are available here, but ideally you could also just guess and be fine." Different users resonate more with one or the other.

284
00:26:34,149 --> 00:26:37,110
You know, some users maybe would never feel like guessing.

285
00:26:37,110 --> 00:26:39,350
Maybe that's just not their thing, right?

286
00:26:39,350 --> 00:26:45,269
And sometimes you would never guess the thing because it's an operation that only exists on that one object, so you never would have seen it before, right?

287
00:26:45,269 --> 00:26:46,789
So yeah, I think there's a use case for both.

288
00:26:46,799 --> 00:26:50,390
Do you have any specific tips for documenting or describing APIs?

289
00:26:50,390 --> 00:26:59,350
And as I'm thinking about this, is it better to have like separate documents for that or would you rather interrogate the API to figure out what's there or do we need both?

290
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:01,510
I've done different things over time.

291
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:09,190
These days I lean towards using open API to write a spec basically that describes what the API should be doing.

292
00:27:09,190 --> 00:27:16,789
And then from there, you know, there's a lot of great tooling that will generate nice HTML documentation from that that you can give out to users.

293
00:27:16,789 --> 00:27:23,750
And then we even use a lot of tooling to take that same spec to be able to run our test against it.

294
00:27:23,750 --> 00:27:35,990
So we actually have in testing basically like if you make an API call that does not somehow match with the spec that will just raise an error automatically so that we can make sure that basically like what we're documenting lines up with what we're testing which lines up with what we're actually presenting.

295
00:27:35,990 --> 00:27:45,909
And that has been really helpful to have that be something that's basically machine readable because then you can do interesting things with it as opposed to literally just a markdown file somewhere that says what it does, right?

296
00:27:45,909 --> 00:27:49,029
which is much more prone to drifting away from what is in actuality happening.

297
00:27:49,039 --> 00:27:52,070
Yeah, stale docs definitely cause a lot of problems.

298
00:27:52,080 --> 00:28:05,110
Yes, for sure. Going back to our principle of least surprise a little bit, how do you balance being new and innovative and doing things in a different way and sticking to what people are used to and what they expect? Give an example of that.

299
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:07,110
Sure.

300
00:28:07,110 --> 00:28:09,029
I mean, I wouldn't say that I've always gotten that balance correct.

301
00:28:09,029 --> 00:28:13,510
I mean, I think some of it goes to what the needs of the user are.

302
00:28:13,510 --> 00:28:20,310
Like I think doing anything innovative, you know, is highly unlikely to be something that will fit into the principle of least surprise, right?

303
00:28:20,310 --> 00:28:23,430
Like if they've never seen it before, they're not going to guess it.

304
00:28:23,430 --> 00:28:25,751
It's just not how that works, right? So there's a couple parts to it.

305
00:28:25,760 --> 00:28:31,029
One is obviously like if you are going to do something new, at least do it consistently, right?

306
00:28:31,029 --> 00:28:38,950
Don't just do something new in one place and do something else that is new to do the same kind of thing somewhere else, right?

307
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:40,549
Like try to have a pattern basically.

308
00:28:40,549 --> 00:28:45,590
So that's one of the things that I guess I lean towards is like try to have a new pattern, not a new one-off, right?

309
00:28:45,590 --> 00:28:50,310
So like what we were talking about with like having the actions be nested and grouped together, right?

310
00:28:50,310 --> 00:28:51,830
That's a pattern that you can repeat, you know?

311
00:28:51,830 --> 00:28:54,789
So you could have anytime there are actions anywhere in the API, they appear like this, right?

312
00:28:54,799 --> 00:28:59,669
And here's the set of rules that apply to actions so you can understand what to expect there.

313
00:28:59,669 --> 00:29:03,029
But yeah, I think some of it just comes down to like how much pain is this causing or not?

314
00:29:03,029 --> 00:29:04,630
How much confusion is it causing or not?

315
00:29:04,630 --> 00:29:08,230
Is it worth trying to do something a little bit different to ease that?

316
00:29:08,230 --> 00:29:15,990
And also there's a lot of cases where more and more I lean towards doing the again both and thing, right, of like maybe I can provide the old way and the new way.

317
00:29:15,990 --> 00:29:19,430
And so then I can make an argument for why the new way is better.

318
00:29:19,430 --> 00:29:24,630
And if people are actually interested and buy that argument and have those needs, they'll probably use the new way. And if they don't, they can still use the old way.

319
00:29:24,640 --> 00:29:26,389
Like it's not, you know, all or nothing.

320
00:29:26,399 --> 00:29:33,909
Like you have to get ramped up to my weird thing that might or might not be good and let's, you know, in order to even use my API.

321
00:29:33,909 --> 00:29:39,990
Would an example of that maybe be like running a GraphQL server and a regular CRUD server side by side or something like that?

322
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:44,870
Yeah, quite possibly. And I mean sometimes that's just not reasonable or realistic, right?

323
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:47,669
Because the operational cost of running more than one thing is high.

324
00:29:47,669 --> 00:29:55,669
Some of the assumptions that CRUD versus GraphQL make incompatible in terms of how you're even storing the data, right? Like things like that.

325
00:29:55,669 --> 00:29:57,510
But yeah, I think that that could be a reasonable example.

326
00:29:57,520 --> 00:29:59,430
You mentioned being a Ruby developer.

327
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:02,310
You have a Ruby gem called Fog.

328
00:30:02,310 --> 00:30:04,470
Do you want to talk about that a little bit and what it does?

329
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:08,070
Sure.

330
00:30:08,070 --> 00:30:10,790
This is kind of the from the early days of cloud. I was a Ruby developer.

331
00:30:10,790 --> 00:30:19,110
I was working at cloud infrastructure startups and I was finding that you know in

332
00:30:19,110 --> 00:30:28,389
when AWS even was quite new and there were some other competitors just coming online that the clients available to me to interact with those were left a lot to be desired.

333
00:30:28,389 --> 00:30:30,389
I guess they either were non-existent or just not very good yet.

334
00:30:30,399 --> 00:30:39,510
And so I initially set out just to add clients for a couple of the really new AWS services that I didn't see any clients for yet.

335
00:30:39,510 --> 00:30:46,870
I think I want to say maybe Simple DB or something was the first one that I did, which I've not even heard anybody talk about in a really long time, I don't think.

336
00:30:46,870 --> 00:30:49,750
But at the time I was just like this is interesting.

337
00:30:49,750 --> 00:30:56,630
And so yeah, I kind of like dived into writing these clients just because cloud seemed exciting and I wanted to learn more and I wanted to be able to use it.

338
00:30:56,630 --> 00:30:58,470
And again, I just the tools weren't there.

339
00:30:58,470 --> 00:31:04,630
So, at first it was just like me dabbling with that and then over time it grew to be a lot of clients for a lot of different APIs.

340
00:31:04,630 --> 00:31:12,470
As my interest continued to grow where people asked me if I could add support for something else and then eventually I worked

341
00:31:12,470 --> 00:31:20,711
to start building abstractions on top of that so that you could have some degree of interoperability sort of like the adapter pattern basically of like saying I would like a server. Okay.

342
00:31:20,711 --> 00:31:27,830
well like which cloud provider would you like a server on and have it be able to do at least many of the things to make it interoperable between those providers

343
00:31:27,840 --> 00:31:39,190
right so it would understand that when you say you want a server on Rackspace it's this API call that you need to make but if you want a server on AWS it's this other different API call that you might need to make that is definitely something

344
00:31:39,190 --> 00:31:51,110
that I've struggled with a lot is having things that objects that do similar things but have different APIs and trying to come up with good abstractions around that what lessons have you learned from fog that might help other people with Sure.

345
00:31:51,110 --> 00:31:55,750
It depends a lot on the nature of the objects, I guess.

346
00:31:55,750 --> 00:32:02,950
And I think this kind of relates maybe also to the action pattern I was talking about actually like was which is something I kind of like stumbled upon much later.

347
00:32:02,950 --> 00:32:10,950
But the closer it is to basically just CRUD, the easier it is going to be to have something that is more interoperable like a repeatable pattern.

348
00:32:10,950 --> 00:32:13,190
So a good example of that would be like cloud storage, right?

349
00:32:13,190 --> 00:32:18,549
Whether it's S3 or the many other S3 clones and competitors that now exist, right?

350
00:32:18,549 --> 00:32:30,470
like the interface to most of them is pretty comparable, you know, like you create folders to contain files, you create files within those folders, you fetch them, you update them, you know, like that's kind of it, right?

351
00:32:30,470 --> 00:32:34,149
So creating a more uniform interface to all of those is at least relatively straightforward.

352
00:32:34,159 --> 00:32:37,909
The thing where much trickier is like the example I gave of like servers.

353
00:32:37,909 --> 00:32:40,630
When people say server, they don't always even seem to mean the same thing, right?

354
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:45,670
Like there's a lot of different things that could mean in terms of like is it bare metal? Is it virtualized?

355
00:32:45,670 --> 00:32:47,269
Is it somewhere in between?

356
00:32:47,269 --> 00:32:49,990
Does it have volumes that are attached that you need to do something with?

357
00:32:49,990 --> 00:32:52,549
Do you like put an image onto it?

358
00:32:52,549 --> 00:32:55,269
Do you put a a Docker thing onto it? Like, you know, there's a lot of different things it could mean.

359
00:32:55,279 --> 00:33:04,549
So, the challenge there definitely be became like it felt like the interface that resulted was sort of this lowest common denominator thing where I mean technically it can boot a server,

360
00:33:04,549 --> 00:33:14,070
but is it like actually a meaningful useful server if you use the generic interface or do you have to get into all these particulars for that particular service in order to actually get it to do what you want?

361
00:33:14,070 --> 00:33:16,711
At which point, how much value is this really adding? So, I don't know.

362
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:22,870
Like, it was definitely an interesting thing to explore and there are parts of it that work great, but there's other parts that Yeah.

363
00:33:22,870 --> 00:33:28,470
just because the services are specialized and they're trying to differentiate from each other or whatever, they keep pulling away from you all the time.

364
00:33:28,470 --> 00:33:32,149
So, it's hard to just like keep them close enough to continue to be meaningful and interesting.

365
00:33:32,149 --> 00:33:39,750
I run into that a lot in my work because I have a variety of instruments and like say a digital multimeter and people choose specific multimeters

366
00:33:39,750 --> 00:33:49,110
because they want this one specific piece of functionality and then if you put that in the generic interface then you run into problems and you're breaking all the solid rules or whatever and things like that.

367
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:54,789
Yeah. Yeah. It's that's where it gets really tricky is yeah when there's differentiation like how do you deal with that?

368
00:33:54,789 --> 00:33:57,909
And of course these are competing companies so they're trying really hard to differentiate.

369
00:33:57,909 --> 00:34:05,830
So, it's like this never- ending battle basically to try to figure out some way to make all of those pieces fit together as they continue to try to pull apart.

370
00:34:05,840 --> 00:34:11,270
Let's change topics a little bit and talk about anchor and local.host.

371
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:16,790
Sure. Can you talk a little bit about what Ankor is and what it does and what need you're trying to fill?

372
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:20,950
Relates to our discussion earlier of how OpenSSL is kind of a beast to work with.

373
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:33,510
you know, like increasingly people see the need and value to do encryption and to have certificates, but they're still kind of a pain to actually work on and work with.

374
00:34:33,510 --> 00:34:42,310
And so the goal with Ankor is really to make them more accessible and easy to use so that everybody can encrypt whenever and wherever they need to.

375
00:34:42,310 --> 00:34:48,950
And so we're slowly but surely trying to chip away at that problem and provide more, you know, capabilities to do that more readily.

376
00:34:48,950 --> 00:34:52,710
I think ease of use is really important when it comes to security stuff like this.

377
00:34:52,710 --> 00:35:06,231
I was listening to a somebody talk about security the other day and she mentioned that a simple thing you can do is when you Google like how to do something to just add a how to do it securely onto the end because everybody always puts like the easy answer like oh I can't access this file. What should I do?

378
00:35:06,231 --> 00:35:12,630
Oh change the permissions and make them wide open or oh my SSL certificate's not working. Oh well here's how you disable the SSL certificate.

379
00:35:12,640 --> 00:35:14,390
Yes.

380
00:35:14,390 --> 00:35:18,710
The number of times I've seen the response to security questions be here is how you turn off HTTPS.

381
00:35:18,710 --> 00:35:21,109
I can't even begin to count.

382
00:35:21,109 --> 00:35:22,790
I mean, and it just kind of hurts me.

383
00:35:22,790 --> 00:35:31,590
Makes me die a little bit more inside every time I see that, you know, because it's not a good sustainable reasonable approach, you know, but it gets the problem solved quickly. So, it's tempting.

384
00:35:31,590 --> 00:35:34,790
So, yeah. Or like turn off the firewall or something similar.

385
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:41,430
Yep. For sure. Those are often those are good as a first step just to see like, okay, that's what's causing my problem.

386
00:35:41,430 --> 00:35:44,151
But if you stop there and say, "Oh, I solve my problem." You walk away.

387
00:35:44,151 --> 00:35:45,910
Well, you've just created more problems.

388
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:53,750
Yeah, for sure. So, how does your you have some command line tools and stuff to make things easier for developers.

389
00:35:53,750 --> 00:35:57,910
How does it help solve that problem of like, oh, I could just do this?

390
00:35:57,910 --> 00:36:01,510
Is there an equally easy solution with your command line tools to solve some of those problems?

391
00:36:01,520 --> 00:36:08,069
Yeah, in particular with the Anchor CLI, we've developed what we call LCL.host.

392
00:36:08,079 --> 00:36:17,750
And so the idea with that is to try as much as possible to make it really easy for you to get encryption working on your local machine.

393
00:36:17,750 --> 00:36:27,589
So you're you know you're running your app server in whichever language and you want it to serve secure content to your browser on your machine.

394
00:36:27,589 --> 00:36:35,430
Generally this is uh challenging because a lot of the ways that you would get certificates normally like let's encrypt or things like that you need to do DNS management and other things

395
00:36:35,430 --> 00:36:42,790
and like you know most people probably don't necessarily have DNS pointing to their local machine in a way that they can easily do that and so historically

396
00:36:42,790 --> 00:36:57,190
you would basically have to provision the dreaded self-signed searchs you know which again you have to like use cryptic commands to actually generate them but then can also be challenging because now you've solved that problem for you but if you're on a team of developers which most people probably are.

397
00:36:57,190 --> 00:36:58,710
This is still a problem for everyone else on the team, right?

398
00:36:58,720 --> 00:37:00,470
And there's a lot of examples.

399
00:37:00,470 --> 00:37:10,390
For instance, if you're trying to use like Stripe or Apple Pay or something and you're trying to develop on that, like you can't actually use those interfaces in your browser if it's not a secure context, right?

400
00:37:10,390 --> 00:37:13,670
So, it just doesn't work.

401
00:37:13,670 --> 00:37:16,790
So, the idea with a lot of the LCL host stuff is to make it as easy as possible to do that.

402
00:37:16,790 --> 00:37:27,190
So we have a single command basically that you can run that just goes through the steps that are necessary to provision a certificate for your machine to install it where it needs to be in your trust store so that your browser will recognize

403
00:37:27,190 --> 00:37:39,030
and use it and other tools will recognize and use it and then you know within just a few minutes you're up and running and you can see again whatever your web app is delivered through a secure context so that you can get back to doing whatever you need to do.

404
00:37:39,040 --> 00:37:43,829
So previously we talked about web APIs a lot.

405
00:37:43,829 --> 00:37:47,430
Do you see a major difference between web APIs and CLI APIs?

406
00:37:47,440 --> 00:37:49,510
Sometimes.

407
00:37:49,510 --> 00:37:51,510
I mean, it depends a lot on just like what the use cases are.

408
00:37:51,510 --> 00:38:02,310
So, you know, there are parts of our API that we've developed basically because, you know, like the LCL command that we were just talking about is trying to accomplish certain things.

409
00:38:02,310 --> 00:38:09,030
I'm not sure if end users normally would be trying to do those things or to be doing them in quite the same way.

410
00:38:09,030 --> 00:38:16,790
So I think it might make sense to for instance provide the normal CRUD operations across your different resources because you know eventually everybody's going to need them

411
00:38:16,790 --> 00:38:26,310
or whatever but maybe to have particular actions or additional endpoints that are specifically for the CLI because again it just needs to do things sometimes that other people don't necessarily need to do or care to do.

412
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:28,550
And so I don't know it's just a mix.

413
00:38:28,560 --> 00:38:31,750
It's just whatever is needed for one or the other.

414
00:38:31,750 --> 00:38:40,630
I think the other thing is like we have for better or worse designed certain CLI actions that actually require multiple API calls bene be behind the scenes

415
00:38:40,630 --> 00:38:47,910
and that can get hairy because now it's not impossible but it's very difficult to have transactional boundaries around all those actions right so it's very easy

416
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:59,190
then to end up in situations where things end up in weird partial states right because two of the changes succeeded and then the third one failed and so now do you roll that all back or something

417
00:38:59,190 --> 00:39:09,190
whereas if you just created a single API endpoint point that was basically directly tied to that particular CLI action, it would be much easier to wrap all of the things in transaction boundary. Does that make sense?

418
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:11,030
Yeah.

419
00:39:11,030 --> 00:39:16,551
If I'm understanding correctly, in this particular case, your CLI is actually calling an API behind the scenes, like a web API. Okay.

420
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:18,551
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

421
00:39:18,551 --> 00:39:20,470
So, we definitely have cases where Yeah.

422
00:39:20,470 --> 00:39:24,390
the CLI itself is calling a web API and doing multiple different things.

423
00:39:24,390 --> 00:39:26,069
And usually from the way it's set up, that's just fine.

424
00:39:26,069 --> 00:39:39,190
But yeah, there are some edge cases where we've ended up in outliers where yeah, there's stuff that's in weird state because only some of the commands succeeded because we don't have a specific API on the website that is wrapping all of that in a transaction.

425
00:39:39,200 --> 00:39:41,589
So they don't match one to one necessarily.

426
00:39:41,599 --> 00:39:43,191
Yeah. Yeah.

427
00:39:43,191 --> 00:39:47,349
There's a lot of cases where the CLI is actually doing a few things for you instead of just one big thing.

428
00:39:47,359 --> 00:39:56,630
So is that the case of then giving them the easy solution that is run this one CLI command versus making several different API calls for the more advanced user?

429
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:58,310
Yeah, that is definitely the case.

430
00:39:58,310 --> 00:40:06,550
And I mean we've already seen that in some cases too where it's sort of like we are doing a somewhat more complicated thing in the CLI to make it easier for the users.

431
00:40:06,550 --> 00:40:11,510
But now we're starting to identify that oh actually this is just something that users are going to want to do more generally.

432
00:40:11,510 --> 00:40:19,190
And so now we're moving towards developing some new API endpoints that will do the more complicated thing that the CLI used to do

433
00:40:19,190 --> 00:40:28,069
and then move the CLI to use that endpoint instead of again like having it do four operations and take all the return values from all of that and knit them together in some particular way and then give you the output from that.

434
00:40:28,069 --> 00:40:31,109
You know, it's like maybe that should just be one API call that does all of that.

435
00:40:31,119 --> 00:40:35,910
So, you know, we continue to learn, we continue to make mistakes, we continue to tweak and iterate.

436
00:40:35,910 --> 00:40:37,910
Again, you know, trying to be agile like we talked about.

437
00:40:37,920 --> 00:40:49,670
that kind of gels with this idea that I've heard a lot lately of pushing the complexity down and making things easier in the sense that you know the typical use case is to do these three things all in order

438
00:40:49,670 --> 00:40:53,430
and it's it's like the 90% use case make a method or make an action that does just that.

439
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:55,190
Yeah.

440
00:40:55,190 --> 00:41:04,550
And I don't know that I have gotten all the way into doing that in the web API as much as I might like, but we've definitely done that a lot in the CLI, I would say, where for instance the LCL command that we mentioned,

441
00:41:04,550 --> 00:41:10,069
like behind the scenes, it actually runs, I think it's either four or five CLI commands.

442
00:41:10,069 --> 00:41:15,830
And you can technically run each of those in order yourself, but like in many cases, why would you, right? Like let the tool do this for me.

443
00:41:15,839 --> 00:41:26,630
And you know just generally I think I think more and more about this notion of things like CRUD things like actions but also having other action other possibilities that are more like a workflow almost

444
00:41:26,630 --> 00:41:35,910
right like so that's the sort of notion of like there's actually these three actions that people very commonly want to do together so maybe we should have a sort of workflow that encompasses those set of things

445
00:41:35,910 --> 00:41:43,589
and just can run end to end and maybe it includes some hints or extra information that you wouldn't get if you ran them individually because this is a broader thing

446
00:41:43,589 --> 00:41:58,069
or maybe it doesn't I don't know but it gives you some context to provide something specifically to suit that use case in a way that is more tailored that provides a nicer experience that gives the person just what they need especially again when it's a super common use case that happens day in day out.

447
00:41:58,079 --> 00:42:00,710
Yeah.

448
00:42:00,710 --> 00:42:04,790
So maybe they do step one and then it prompts do you want to continue to step two or you can just cancel out or something like that.

449
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:12,309
Yeah. Yeah. So wrapping things up talk that I heard you give recently you mentioned an HTTP API design guide.

450
00:42:12,319 --> 00:42:14,309
Mhm.

451
00:42:14,309 --> 00:42:15,910
Where can people get that and what is it and what does it cover?

452
00:42:15,920 --> 00:42:19,430
Sure.

453
00:42:19,430 --> 00:42:26,630
It's on the inter agent in ter agent GitHub and it's was from when I worked at Heroku.

454
00:42:26,630 --> 00:42:35,910
So at Heroku I was the lead designer for the version 3 API and so coming out of that we tried to basically kind of like create like a style guide.

455
00:42:35,910 --> 00:42:39,670
I don't know if you're familiar with the notion of style guides from say like writing or whatever.

456
00:42:39,680 --> 00:42:50,390
There's the is it the Chicago style guide and there's like the AP style guide and things like that that kind of say like here's when you should or shouldn't use commas or apostrophes or dashes or you know things like that.

457
00:42:50,390 --> 00:42:58,471
So the idea was kind of trying to copy from something like that and at least in part it was like geared towards us doing that internally. I've designed this API.

458
00:42:58,471 --> 00:43:05,510
I I now want to try to explain to you how and why I've done certain things and how you should do them so that it'll be easier for other people to contribute

459
00:43:05,520 --> 00:43:13,829
and participate and for us to continue to have something that's consistent to have less surprises, better principle of least surprise, things like that.

460
00:43:13,829 --> 00:43:21,030
And having done that, we decided that a lot of the advice was pretty generically useful and interesting and so it would be good to share that more broadly.

461
00:43:21,030 --> 00:43:24,230
And so we opened it up and released it to the public.

462
00:43:24,230 --> 00:43:27,589
And yeah, it tries to cover just a lot of things like talks about the actions pattern that I mentioned.

463
00:43:27,589 --> 00:43:29,910
It talks about,

464
00:43:29,910 --> 00:43:42,950
you know, versioning and ideas and recommendations for that and just kind of tries to cover a lot of different things of how to try to again have an API that's consistent that people can kind of understand and navigate that doesn't have too much surprise ideally.

465
00:43:42,950 --> 00:43:48,870
Yeah, just first principles for a lot of that stuff to hopefully help make it easier for people to I guess learn from my taste.

466
00:43:48,870 --> 00:43:50,470
It's a cookbook almost or something, right?

467
00:43:50,470 --> 00:43:57,829
of sort of like I have developed this taste for APIs or whatever and I'm trying to explain to someone how and why you might want to follow along with those examples.

468
00:43:57,839 --> 00:44:03,270
Do you have any idea of the adoption of this guide? Like do you know anyone who's been using it?

469
00:44:03,280 --> 00:44:07,270
I mean got a bunch of stars I guess on GitHub.

470
00:44:07,270 --> 00:44:10,230
That's like a good sign probably.

471
00:44:10,230 --> 00:44:19,829
I mean I've been told by people that they found it very interesting and influential at the time because not a lot of other people had tried to talk openly about that kind of stuff, right?

472
00:44:19,829 --> 00:44:22,950
like you could go and see what somebody's API was, but you didn't really see an explanation of how or why.

473
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:30,790
And so I gather it was influential, but yeah, like just through anecdotes really and I guess through the star counts. So I hope it helps people.

474
00:44:30,790 --> 00:44:32,790
I mean that's why we do stuff like that.

475
00:44:32,790 --> 00:44:35,030
So great.

476
00:44:35,030 --> 00:44:37,670
Is there anything else about APIs that we haven't talked about that you would like to mention?

477
00:44:37,680 --> 00:44:41,910
I mean another relating to open API and things like that too.

478
00:44:41,910 --> 00:44:48,550
The other part of that that I found to be quite useful that we didn't talk about is mocking, which is if you have an open API spec,

479
00:44:48,560 --> 00:45:03,030
there are a lot of nice tools actually that will basically start a server that will provide all of the API endpoints that you have defined that you can actually start to write a client against and get back not real but realistic responses.

480
00:45:03,030 --> 00:45:05,589
I found that to be super helpful.

481
00:45:05,589 --> 00:45:09,349
Again, going to the notion of trying to make the mistakes in the cheapest way possible.

482
00:45:09,349 --> 00:45:17,910
So a lot of my flow now is sort of like write a spec that is what I think I need then start to write a client against it and that usually helps me

483
00:45:17,910 --> 00:45:27,109
to reveal quite quickly what I forgot what I overlooked and then adjusting the spec and then actually go into the real implementation of it once that spec is settled down.

484
00:45:27,109 --> 00:45:31,750
It isn't a way that I had developed historically and having stumbled upon that I found it really useful.

485
00:45:31,750 --> 00:45:33,589
So I think that's a good one to keep in mind for people.

486
00:45:33,589 --> 00:45:38,550
So in that case then you write the client or a sample client before you actually write the server.

487
00:45:38,550 --> 00:45:40,790
Is that Yes.

488
00:45:40,790 --> 00:45:51,430
Um for sure because again like for me yeah I guess I don't want to end up in the situation where I like do all of the work of writing the server code and then realize that I had like fundamental misunderstandings about what I was trying to do.

489
00:45:51,430 --> 00:45:53,270
And so doing the client will often help me to get there faster.

490
00:45:53,280 --> 00:45:55,829
Right?

491
00:45:55,829 --> 00:46:04,150
So whether that's doing some in my case again I was working on CLI stuff a I would actually like start to write out the CLI operation and make it work at least in the

492
00:46:04,150 --> 00:46:14,710
right shape of things working before I'd ever implemented the server and that would quickly reveal to me, oh, I left out two or three fields that really ought to be here or, you know, this doesn't get me this piece of information that I needed.

493
00:46:14,710 --> 00:46:16,950
So, do I add this to this API endpoint?

494
00:46:16,950 --> 00:46:21,750
Do I like make another call to another API endpoint? Do I make a new API endpoint that includes both of them?

495
00:46:21,750 --> 00:46:25,430
Like, you know, what do I need to do to get to where I need to be?

496
00:46:25,430 --> 00:46:38,630
definitely has helped me to again be more agile basically because I can iterate before doing the server implementation as well like because at the end of the day you're going to have to implement the server and the client and probably the spec and so yeah that just feels like the faster way.

497
00:46:38,630 --> 00:46:43,109
Usually the client is simpler in my experience than the server is.

498
00:46:43,109 --> 00:46:57,670
So work on the spec first which is it's the simplest and cheapest then the client second because it's the next simplest and cheapest I guess and then defer the server till the last because you have the most information then so you least likely to make mistakes you know more likely to be able to just push through and get it done.

499
00:46:57,680 --> 00:47:00,470
That sounds like great advice. Thank you.

